lately I've been pushing the idea of  incidental training the idea is that you  perform exercises wherever you are  whatever you're doing that might mean  that you make a movement more difficult  by pistol squatting down to the ground  to pick something up instead of bending  over or it might mean that you just do  five pull-ups when you're walking  through any doorway and thereby use  greasing the groove to improve your  pull-up technique and your max count you  can stretch whilst watching TV you can  do curls off the kettle's boiling and by  constantly moving your body like this  like an animal you'll build far more  strength far more endurance and feel  better than ever most of us know this  intuitively or we at least know that we  should train more so why don't we  exercise as much as we know we should  the problem is for lot of us that we  just lack invigoration we just feel  tired we feel exhausted all the time if  I told you right now that I wanted you  to go and do some spider-man crawls in  the garden outside just 20 then maybe  the very thought of that would make your  toes curl you might feel weak you might  feel off balance you might feel too  tired what's the reason for this well I  assumed that it had to do with just  being overtired or overstressed but I  did some research and actually the  studies tend to show that being tired is  in sleep deprived doesn't make us any  physically weaker it only slightly hurts  our endurance and this comes down water  psychological factors such as motivation  so if being tired doesn't make you  weaker then why do you feel stiff and  achy and lethargic whenever you think of  working out why do you have to G  yourself up to one big workout why does  it take ages to rev yourself up to it  like a cold engine the reason is just  that you are like a cold engine you're  not exercising enough through the day  you're not even moving enough through  the day if you spend all day sitting at  a desk sitting on the sofa standing at  the kitchen you're barely moving your  body and what happens is you actually  lose that connection to the muscles they  build up a kind of residual tension more  tonus than is needed to just maintain  your posture this is made worse by  psychological stress which most of us go  through in droves each day and by the  fact that we're just so out of practice  eventually this lack of movement can  lead to a complete loss of awareness of  specific muscle groups leading to  awkward patterns of activation that  cause tightness poor movement patterns  and discomfort this can be both acute or  chronic  the latter is sometimes referred to as  sensory motor amnesia which is a term  coined by Thomas Hannah creator of  cymatics warming up was a nice idea in  theory is something that no animal in  the  or kingdom ever does you never see a dog  limber up before it chases prey if it  had to the prey would get away if you  think about any animal if you have a pet  then you'll notice that when they wake  up in the morning the first thing they  do isn't to bend over and touch their  toes and hold it for two minutes no  animal in the animal kingdom does that  instead they stretch out their spine  just for a couple of seconds you see  them reach up to the sky like they're  doing yoga and then get on with their  day they're not actually stretching here  what they're actually doing is something  called panda chelation panda q Latian is  slightly different from a stretch in a  stretch you move the limb or the body  part outside of your normal range of  motion that causes a contraction in the  muscle a stretch reflex and you have to  try and resist this in order to increase  your mobility this does nothing to  relieve tension has nothing to do with  your figuration on the other hand panda  chelation means that you're tensing the  muscle and then lengthening it and then  releasing it this is what happens when  you yawn or you stretch when you yawn  that's actually appendicular of the  respiratory muscles and what's happening  is that you're contracting and  lengthening your jaw muscles your neck  muscles and even your intercostal  muscles this is a kind of stretch but it  has a different purpose the idea here is  to remind to fire up that part of the  motor cortex to remind it where that  muscle group is to give it more control  over it to prime it ready for movement  and at the same time to listen to the  feedback from the muscle through gain  that sensory input because your muscles  remember our sensory organs they provide  us with proprioceptive your muscle  spindles and your Goldie tendon organ  our body uses these to know where it is  in space to know how tense you are to  know your position your posture to  create that mental map of your body so  like I say when you don't use the muscle  all day you actually forget how to use  it and at the same time you actually get  a buildup of tension which makes it much  harder to activate in future you feel  stiff you feel tense you feel tired and  when you think about moving you feel  wobbly and offset and jittery by  performing these pandit collations what  you're doing is reminding your body  where the muscle is and also releasing  that tension by consciously relaxing the  muscle so don't just yawn yawn with your  whole body stretch out your toes in the  morning stretch out your arms stretch  out your chest push your chest together  just contract and stretch if you  struggle to visualize what this should  be like then try yawning at the same  time you'll get more of an idea it  should feel good it shouldn't feel like  an amazing release so next time you're  thinking ma'am I could do 10 push-ups  now  don't have that energy or that sense of  control do something dick ulation for  your chest for your shoulders for your  legs stretch yourself out yawn suddenly  you might feel that you have the in  figuration you need to go ahead and do  it this is also fantastic before bed  because it helps to relax your muscles  you'll sleep far better once again just  like your dog or cat which never seems  to have any trouble waking up in the  night to go to the toilet and perhaps  the best part of all is that if you do  this regularly throughout the day and  make it habit then you'll eventually  start to improve your proprioceptive  because you're doing all the time on a  habitual basis you gain permanent  greater control over that muscle and  permanent greater connection to the  sensory information coming in you'll  gain that childlike movements that they  teach in Shaolin you'll be ready like a  coiled spring to launch into action at  any time and you don't have to stretch  and touch your toes for even a second  the other key is to keep moving  throughout the day don't spend 23 hours  sitting completely still and one hour  working out to the gym the aim here is  not to do loads of workouts throughout  the day and intensive ly push yourself  with no chance of recovery  instead we're simply moving the muscles  to remember they're there to cement new  skills and possibly even to slightly  improve muscle protein synthesis the  world around us has been designed to  make movement unnecessary everything is  at the right height for us many tasks  can be completed while sitting we need  to move in spite of this back and  thereby keep our bodies limber fired up  and ready to go whenever we need them  basically this becomes a virtuous cycle  you're probably just out of the habit of  moving but the more you move the easier  it becomes to exercise when you want to  actually have a workout and that in turn  means you're more likely to workout  which makes it easier to move whenever  you want to so give it a go let me know  what you think if this is something you  do already then comment down below and  let me know if it's worked for you and I  look forward to reading your comments  hope you enjoyed this quick little tip  stay tuned for more like this and much  bigger videos on the way soon subscribe  as well of course if you want to get  more and check out the links in the  description down below for my ebook and  program as well as the website the  Pioneer thanks a ton for watching and  bye for now

